WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.432
Latency, measured the way users feel it.

2
00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:13.912
Report latency as percentiles, at least p50 (the median) and p95 (the slowest one in twenty), because averages hide the tail and users notice the tail.

3
00:00:14.192 --> 00:00:24.987
For interactive features, measure time to first token separately from total time; for extraction, where your code needs the whole JSON object, total time is what matters.

4
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:28.765
Measure under realistic conditions, or the numbers mean little.

5
00:00:29.045 --> 00:00:32.416
Call the API from the region your application runs in.

6
00:00:32.696 --> 00:00:45.795
Load the self-hosted server with the concurrency you expect in production, because a server that answers one request in 300 milliseconds may take several seconds per request when forty arrive at once.

7
00:00:46.075 --> 00:00:50.213
Warm up first, so model loading and cache misses don’t land in your sample.

8
00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:56.872
Record the hardware, the serving engine and its version, quantization, and batch settings alongside the numbers.

9
00:00:57.152 --> 00:01:08.245
Independent benchmarkers such as Artificial Analysis publish their latency definitions for exactly this reason: two “latency” numbers measured differently can’t be compared.

10
00:01:08.525 --> 00:01:11.747
Latency is also where the systems often differ most.

11
00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:24.464
A small model on a dedicated GPU, close to your application, can answer in well under a second; a frontier API call commonly takes longer, and its tail depends on load you don’t control.

12
00:01:24.744 --> 00:01:34.493
Whether that matters depends on the product: a batch job that processes yesterday’s meetings overnight doesn’t care, and an inline suggestion while someone types does.
